The Windows node's rerun is in. Real-machine problems fell from 236 to 23, and each of the remaining 23 has a named cause. I started a second fix package (WP7.F2) for them and replied to the Windows node with the plan.

**One security gap on the real Windows node.** Rerunning `key init` there did not tighten the private key's permissions on the existing key; WP7.F only made the message about them accurate. Administrators and SYSTEM still have full access to the key. WP7.F2 makes `key init` tighten an existing key too. I told the Windows node not to copy or back up its keys folder until that lands. The six test errors where `ssh-keygen` reports "bad permissions" come from the same cause: some code paths that write key files skip the permission step.

**The fast tier on Windows got slower, not faster:** about 145 s on two runs, up from 74.7 s. The 40 slowest tests account for only 40.5 s, so the rest is spread across all the other tests. Defender scanning the Windows temp folder is the leading guess, but it's unverified. WP7.F2 adds per-module timing and a configurable temp folder (`MVMC_TEST_TMP`) so the next run can settle it.

The rest are test fixtures with Windows-specific premises:
- test file names containing a newline, which NTFS rejects (9 setup errors);
- a file named `end.` that the test cleanup can't delete;
- a few path-separator and path-form assertions;
- a package-digest mismatch I suspect is a line-ending difference, not yet confirmed.

When WP7.F2 lands I'll ask the Windows node to rerun `key init` and the real-machine check. WP7.2 (mirrors) is still running.

